# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not ship as resolved. Reviewed clean workflow-v3 HEAD 7c9f20f against origin/main 40b484b, including the Round 6 delta and hook registration. Explicit verdicts: R-006 incomplete; R-008 incomplete; R-009 incomplete; R-011 incomplete; R-012 fixed for the reported attached/separated -e cases; R-013 fixed for ordinary words, with /w probe/p retained as the accepted, disclosed limitation. New findings: two remaining regression classes below, plus two additional variants of existing findings. Evidence is static inspection and installed GNU sed documentation; no files edited or builds/tests run.

Findings:
- [high] Combined command prefixes still bypass sed protection (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:205-214)
  New regression-class evidence: `command env sed -i 's/x/y/' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` passes classification because resolve_verb removes only `command` and returns `env`. Likewise, `LC_ALL=C command sed -i ...` returns `command`; sudo and xargs wrappers are unrecognized. With sed removed from the coarse filter, these literal protected writes receive no further check. The base filter caught these in-place forms. This violates the unarmed oracle-write boundary and leaves R-008 incomplete.
  Recommendation: Normalize assignments and supported wrappers recursively, respecting option arguments; conservatively inspect wrapped sed invocations when the wrapper cannot be resolved. Add combined-prefix regression cases.
- [high] Single-layer subshell peeling does not restore grouped sed coverage (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:421-431)
  New regression-class evidence: `{ sed -i 's/x/y/' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp; }` resolves the verb as `{`, and `(( sed -i 's/x/y/' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp ))` is not needed to trigger the issue: the valid nested subshell `( (sed -i 's/x/y/' tests/golden/nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp) )` already leaves `(sed` after one peel. Both bypass the classifier, whereas the base substring filter blocked them. Function bodies and command substitutions similarly hide sed behind an unrecognized first token. These are literal writes, distinct from the accepted regex false-block limitation.
  Recommendation: Identify nested executable commands across grouping and substitution boundaries, or retain a conservative sed-aware fallback for unsupported shell structures. Cover braces, nested subshells, functions, substitutions, and background lists.
- [high] Pipeline comments containing separators still corrupt cwd tracking (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:128-145)
  Remaining R-006/R-009 variant: `cd tests/golden; printf x | # note;
cd ../..; printf x > probe.hpp`. Bash ignores the comment's semicolon and runs cd in the pipeline, leaving the parent inside tests/golden. The splitter processes that semicolon before checking the continuation newline, severing pipeline adjacency; it then applies cd ../.. to the parent tracking state and allows the protected redirect. The recorded plain-comment example is fixed, but comment contents remain executable syntax to this scanner.
  Recommendation: Recognize shell comments before processing quotes or control operators, discard their contents through newline, and preserve the pending pipeline boundary. Add a comment-with-semicolon case.
- [high] GNU sed address modifiers defeat write-command detection (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:261-270)
  Remaining R-008/R-011 variant: `cd tests/golden && sed -n '/x/Iw probe.hpp' /dev/null`. GNU sed accepts /x/I as a case-insensitive address; its installed manual states that w destinations are created or truncated before input is read. The bare-write regex rejects w because its predecessor is the address modifier I, and the substitution regex does not match. Thus the hook classifies a file-truncating command as read-only. `/x/Is/x/y/gw probe.hpp` likewise defeats both expressions. This is a write bypass, not the disclosed /w probe/p false block.
  Recommendation: Recognize command positions after valid sed addresses, including I/M modifiers, before classifying w/W and substitution flags. Add addressed-write cases using empty input to cover initialization-time truncation.

